{"name":"Oak Palace","hex":"#bb6b41","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Noakhali."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous tree with distinctive deeply lobed leaves, acorns, and notably strong wood, typically of England and northeastern North America, included in genus Quercus.","The wood of the oak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oak"},{"term":"palace","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Official residence of a head of state or other dignitary, especially in a monarchical or imperial governmental system.","A large and lavishly ornate residence."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate or ornate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/palace"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak","wikidata":"Q12004"},{"title":"Palace","blocks":[{"text":"A palace is a large residence, often serving as a royal residence or the home for a head of state or another high-ranking dignitary, such as a bishop or archbishop. The word is derived from the Latin word palātium, for Palatine Hill in Rome, which housed the Imperial residences."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/